    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT & PRINCIPLE:\nBrown ring test for inorganic nitrate ion qualitative identification.\n\n2. GIVEN INFORMATION / DATA:\nNitrate solution + freshly prepared $FeSO_4$ solution, carefully layered with concentrated $H_2SO_4$ along the test tube wall.\n\n3. FORMULA / EQUATION:\n$NO_3^- + 3Fe^{2+} + 4H^+ \\rightarrow NO + 3Fe^{3+} + 2H_2O$; $[Fe(H_2O)_6]^{2+} + NO \\rightarrow [Fe(H_2O)_5(NO)]^{2+} + H_2O$.\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP REASONING & CALCULATION:\n1. Concentrated sulfuric acid ($H_2SO_4$) reduces nitrate ion ($NO_3^-$) to nitric oxide ($NO$) via oxidation of $Fe^{2+}$ to $Fe^{3+}$.\n2. The unoxidized hydrated ferrous ions $[Fe(H_2O)_6]^{2+}$ present in solution combine rapidly with the generated nitric oxide ($NO$) ligand.\n3. This forms the brown-colored pentaaquanitrosyliron(II) coordination complex: $[Fe(H_2O)_5(NO)]^{2+}$.\n4. Because concentrated $H_2SO_4$ is denser than the aqueous layer, it forms a separate lower layer.\n5. The coordination complex concentrates at the interface, producing a sharp brown ring.\n\n5. FINAL CONCLUSION:\n$[\\text{Fe}(\\text{H}_2\\text{O})_5(\\text{NO})]^{2+}$\n\n6. OPTION ANALYSIS & COMMON TRAPS:\nThe complex contains exactly five water molecules and one nitrosyl ($NO$) ligand with an overall $+2$ charge.",
