    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT & PRINCIPLE:\nCommon ion effect in selective cation group precipitation.\n\n2. GIVEN INFORMATION / DATA:\nGroup III qualitative inorganic salt analysis.\n\n3. FORMULA / EQUATION:\n$NH_4Cl \\rightarrow NH_4^+ + Cl^-$ (suppresses $NH_4OH \\rightleftharpoons NH_4^+ + OH^-$ so $[OH^-]$ is just enough to exceed $K_{sp}$ of Group III hydroxides).\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP REASONING & CALCULATION:\n1. Group III cations ($Fe^{3+}, Al^{3+}, Cr^{3+}$) have very low solubility products ($K_{sp} \\sim 10^{-38}$) for their respective hydroxides.\n2. Group IV and subsequent cations ($Zn^{2+}, Ni^{2+}, Mg^{2+}$) have much higher $K_{sp}$ values for their hydroxides.\n3. To selectively precipitate only Group III hydroxides without prematurely precipitating Group IV cations, the hydroxide ion concentration $[OH^-]$ must be strictly controlled and kept low.\n4. Adding excess ammonium chloride ($NH_4Cl$) provides a high concentration of common ion $NH_4^+$, which shifts the dissociation of weak base $NH_4OH$ backward via the common ion effect.\n5. The resulting $[OH^-]$ is sufficient to exceed the $K_{sp}$ of Group III hydroxides ($Fe(OH)_3, Al(OH)_3$), while remaining safely below the $K_{sp}$ of Group IV and VI cations.\n\n5. FINAL CONCLUSION:\n$\\text{NH}_4\\text{OH}$ in the presence of $\\text{NH}_4\\text{Cl}$\n\n6. OPTION ANALYSIS & COMMON TRAPS:\n$H_2S + \\text{dil. } HCl$ is for Group II cations; $H_2S + NH_4OH$ is for Group IV cations; $(NH_4)_2CO_3$ is for Group V cations.",
